from pathlib import Path
import json, shutil
root=Path(r'D:\Github\Bipedal_Robot')
base=root/'Documentation/Reports and Papers/Dissertation/Notes/advisor_revision_20261023'
out=base/'LIVE_ASTRA_20261026'
packet=base/'REVIEW_INBOX/REVIEWED_20261026'
sources={p.name:p.read_text(encoding='utf-8') for p in (out/'before').glob('*.tex')}
patches=[]
def change(file,old,new,why):
    assert sources[file].count(old)==1,(file,why,sources[file].count(old))
    patches.append(dict(file=file,old=old,new=new.strip(),reason=why))
    sources[file]=sources[file].replace(old,new.strip())
def para(file,start):
    return next(p for p in sources[file].split('\n\n') if p.startswith(start))
f='60-sns.tex'
change(f,'Graphical Circuit Construction','Block Programming Neural Pathways','Ben\'s requested heading; already applied live, verify rather than repeat.')
change(f,para(f,'The missing step was'),r'''The AnimatLab model established that the combined circuit could generate alternating activity and move the suspended limbs. To investigate the contribution of sensory feedback and actuator mechanics, I needed to vary those components while retaining a specified neural organization and mechanical support condition. Extending that approach to the human-scale Gait2392 model and its BPA-routed robot counterpart also required checking joint directions, muscle paths, actuator forces, and the signals exchanged with the controller.''','Replace vague missing-link paragraph with the concrete experimental need.')
change(f,para(f,'I developed several software tools'),r'''Researchers studying neuromechanical control need to distinguish movement generated by a neural circuit from movement shaped by sensory feedback, actuator mechanics, and external support. For the humanoid models studied here, the unresolved questions were whether a specified circuit retained its behavior when implemented in a different simulator, and how its output changed when coupled to human musculotendon mechanics or artificial muscles. A successful neural trace alone could not answer the mechanical question, and a moving model alone could not establish that the intended neural pathways caused the movement.

I addressed this gap by constructing models in which the neural pathways, actuator law, sensory inputs, and support conditions can be specified and varied, together with tests that distinguish circuit behavior from implementation and coupling errors. The resulting contributions are:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item A custom Li--Deng walker and controller, developed in AnimatLab and implemented in MuJoCo with SNS-Toolbox, with matched implementations of its contact-driven neural core in NumPy and Simulink.
    \item Gait2392-based neuromechanical models with alternative neural controllers, and a robot counterpart with BPA routing and the actuator-force model from Chapter~\ref{ch:muscles}.
    \item The SNS-Simscape block library and GUI for block programming neural pathways, including sensory, neuron, synapse, activation, and custom BPA-force blocks.
    \item A SolidWorks-derived robot assembly in Simscape Multibody coupled to a neural controller and BPA actuators using my force model.
    \item Integration of SNS-Simscape controllers with MuJoCo through the MathWorks bidirectional bridge, together with model-import procedures for musculoskeletal and CAD-derived mechanisms.
    \item A five-stage curriculum and experiment workflow for parameter searches, adjustable support, neural stimuli, pathway interventions, mechanical perturbations, and recording neural and mechanical outputs.
    \item Implementations of literature-derived circuits and selected spiking-neuron counterparts for comparing neural mechanisms and coding assumptions.
\end{enumerate}
Table~\ref{tab:sns_capability} gives selected functional-verification tests and proof-of-concept demonstrations. These establish that the tools can construct, exercise, and compare the specified models. The feedback experiments also provide a limited mechanistic result: removing particular sensory pathways can eliminate stepping in a fixed supported configuration, identifying a dependence that would not be apparent from its intact motion alone.''','Address advisor knowledge-gap request with concrete questions, contributions, and demonstrated answers; retain approved list order.')
change(f,para(f,'Existing software supplied'),r'''Existing software supplied the underlying simulation capabilities. OpenSim provided musculoskeletal geometry, MyoConverter translated models to MuJoCo, and SNS-Toolbox provided neuron and synapse formulations \citep{delp_opensim_2007, caggiano_myosuite_2022, todorov_mujoco_2012, nourse_sns_2023}. Simulink provided graphical block programming, and Simscape Multibody provided mechanical modeling and import of SolidWorks-derived mechanisms. My contributions are the particular model implementations, custom blocks, actuator integrations, and evaluation procedures built with those packages. The SNS-Simscape library makes the neural pathways explicit in a block diagram, while the MuJoCo implementations support repeated scripted evaluations and parameter searches. Matched-input tests check selected circuits before their outputs are interpreted as neural control of a mechanical model.''','Credit existing tools and replace unsupported blanket statements about missing software capabilities.')
change(f,para(f,'The tools connect musculoskeletal'),r'''The tools provide three complementary capabilities: constructing a neuromechanical model, testing its component behavior and signal exchange, and evaluating controlled changes in neural or mechanical conditions. Table~\ref{tab:sns_capability} summarizes representative demonstrations. Figure~\ref{fig:pipeline} places the simulation tools within the longer-term robot-control workflow.''','Replace incomplete-work table framing.')
start=sources[f].index('\\begin{table}'); end=sources[f].index('\\end{table}',start)+len('\\end{table}')
change(f,sources[f][start:end],(packet/'patches/P01_companion_table.tex').read_text(encoding='utf-8'),'Use approved tools-and-demonstrations table; Gait2392 second.')
change(f,para(f,'MyoConverter supplies'),(packet/'patches/P07_conversion_summary.tex').read_text(encoding='utf-8'),'Explain the actual conversion problems and repairs, including Ben\'s reply about the initial robotbody attempt.')
change(f,para(f,'The BPA interface evaluates'),r'''For artificial-muscle simulations, I implemented an actuator-force calculation that evaluates the quasi-static BPA model from Chapter~\ref{ch:muscles} at the current simulated actuator length and commanded pressure. The calculated tensile force acts along the model's specified tendon route. Length, velocity, force, and foot-contact measurements are then returned to the neural controller through sensory channels. This closes the neural-command--actuator-force--sensory-feedback loop. The calculation represents the measured static force dependence; it does not add valve-flow or pressure-transient dynamics. Routing geometry and dynamic force transmission therefore require their own checks beyond the isometric force fit.''','Define the BPA interface explicitly and connect it to the preceding conversion account.')
change(f,para(f,'OpenSim-derived and SolidWorks-derived'),r'''I imported OpenSim-derived and SolidWorks-derived mechanisms into Simscape Multibody and coupled the SolidWorks robot assembly to a neural controller and BPA actuators using my force model. The block library also supports reduced-order demonstrations in which the sensory pathway and mechanical response can be inspected directly. These smaller models provide interpretable checks of the components used in larger assemblies.''','Include the author-confirmed coupled SolidWorks assembly; distinguish representative component examples.')
change(f,para(f,'The library assembles'),r'''The library assembles Ia stretch excitation, reciprocal inhibition, and Ib autogenic inhibition in a reduced-order antagonist knee model (Figure~\ref{fig:simulink_reflex_topology}). A second demonstration tests feedback in an arm holding a beverage container as it is loaded. With the reflex enabled, arm sag was \ang{2.00}, compared with \ang{7.85} when it was disabled. The reduced sag demonstrates that the implemented sensory pathway changes motor activation in a direction that opposes the applied load. It is a functional test of the coupled neural and actuator blocks, rather than an experimental measurement of human-arm behavior.''','Explain what the reflex result establishes, rather than appending a generic outstanding-work warning.')
old=para(f,'The MuJoCo--Simulink bridge')
new=r'''The MathWorks MuJoCo--Simulink bridge exchanges activation commands and mechanical measurements between a Simulink controller and an existing MuJoCo mechanical model. I paired this bridge with SNS-Simscape and tested single-muscle activation, different control rates, and sensor readback against a Python integration. Readback was bit-exact over the scored window. An exported neural network also drove all 92 actuator channels during a \qty{5}{\second} run without afferent return; a separate closed-loop reflex experiment exercised both commands and sensory measurements. These tests verify signal exchange through the interface. Under tonic drive, one Simulink circuit realization reached a fixed point while its NumPy reference oscillated, showing why successful signal exchange must be followed by circuit-specific dynamical checks before transferring tuned parameters.'''
new+='\n\n'+(packet/'patches/EB_feedback_ablation.tex').read_text(encoding='utf-8').strip()
new=new.replace('At fixed controller and drive settings,',r'\subsection{Feedback Contribution at Fixed Controller Settings}\label{sec:sns_feedback_test}'+'\n\nAt fixed controller and drive settings,',1)
change(f,old,new,'Clarify bridge tests and add the reviewed fixed-setting feedback proof of concept.')
change(f,'Capabilities and Remaining Limits','Discussion: What the Tools Establish','Lead the discussion with completed contributions.')
change(f,para(f,'The AnimatLab attempt motivated'),r'''The principal contribution is a set of executable neuromechanical models and experimental tools that make the roles of neural pathways and actuator mechanics accessible to controlled tests. The initial AnimatLab model established the combined walker and CPG organization. The Gait2392 implementations extend controller development to a human-scale model with many joints and actuator channels, while the BPA implementations connect the measured actuator-force model to neural control. SNS-Simscape provides a graphical representation of the pathways, and the curriculum and experiment drivers provide repeatable changes in drive, support, feedback, and perturbation conditions.

The representative results answer different parts of the knowledge gap. Agreement between the NumPy and Simulink rhythm cores establishes that the specified circuit response can be preserved across those implementations under matched inputs. The loaded-arm comparison establishes a corrective effect of feedback in the coupled block model. The fixed-controller W2L ablation shows that its tested stepping behavior depends on contact and Ib-force feedback. The bridge tests establish the command and measurement exchange needed to carry out such experiments across software environments. Together, these results provide an operational method for separating implementation agreement, sensory contribution, and mechanical response.''','Connect each demonstrated result to the knowledge gap.')
change(f,para(f,'The next step is to use these'),r'''Researchers can use these tools to compare candidate neural pathways, actuator descriptions, and support conditions while recording their effects on the same defined outputs. The supported demonstrations establish this experimental capability; they do not establish balance without the support rig or transfer of every controller between models. Chapter~\ref{ch:futurework} discusses extensions to weight-bearing control and hardware. Those extensions build on the models and tests already demonstrated here.''','End on usable contributions, with specific scope rather than an incomplete-project narrative.')
# Keep original shared-RG figure and its label; the approved image is uploaded separately.
start=sources[f].index('\\begin{figure}[p]'); end=sources[f].index('\\end{figure}',start)+len('\\end{figure}')
change(f,sources[f][start:end],(packet/'patches/P04_figure61_block.tex').read_text(encoding='utf-8'),'Restore correct Connor--Ben model and visible A/B labels.')

f='10-introduction.tex'
change(f,para(f,'Attempts to implement'),r'''Researchers implementing neural control models face two related problems. Sensory-afferent findings are distributed across species, preparations, and experimental protocols, so selecting a feedback pathway requires locating its measured properties and the conditions under which they were observed \citep{van_de_crommert_review_1998, windhorst_muscle_2007, windhorst_spinal_2022}. I addressed that evidence-access problem with the Sensory Afferent Database. A second problem arises when a published neural circuit is coupled to a different mechanical model: its behavior depends on the actuator mechanics, sensory signals, support conditions, and numerical implementation as well as on the circuit itself. Published architectures and software supply essential components \citep{rybak_modelling_2006, mccrea_organization_2008, markin_afferent_2010, nourse_sns_2023}, but the behavior of their particular combination must be established. I developed coupled human and artificial-muscle models, graphical neural blocks, and controlled evaluation procedures to distinguish these contributions. Representative tests establish circuit agreement across implementations, corrective reflex behavior, and the dependence of a supported stepping configuration on selected feedback pathways.''','Repair grammar and duplicated text; state evidence and neuromechanical knowledge gaps without claiming no prior human model exists.')
old=next(l for l in sources[f].splitlines() if '\\item \\textbf{Develop tools' in l)
change(f,old,r'''    \item \textbf{Develop and demonstrate neuromechanical modeling tools} by implementing the Li--Deng walker, Gait2392-based models with alternative neural controllers and artificial-muscle actuation, SNS-Simscape blocks for neural pathways, and staged tuning and experiment procedures; verify representative circuit responses, implementation agreement, and neural--mechanical signal exchange (Chapters~\ref{ch:sns} and~\ref{ch:overall}).''','Replace vague framework objective with concrete tools and demonstrations.')

f='70-overall.tex'
change(f,para(f,'The fourth enabler'),r'''The fourth contribution is the set of neuromechanical modeling tools in Chapter~\ref{ch:sns}. The Li--Deng walker, Gait2392-based controller implementations, and BPA-force integrations provide defined combinations of neural and mechanical models. SNS-Simscape makes the neural pathways editable as block diagrams, and the experiment drivers vary support, neural drive, sensory pathways, and external perturbations. These tools address the difficulty of determining whether a change in movement arises from neural organization, actuator mechanics, or implementation differences. Matched rhythm-core tests establish agreement between selected NumPy and Simulink implementations; the loaded-arm demonstration shows corrective feedback; and the fixed-controller W2L ablation shows that the tested contact and force-feedback pathways sustain its supported stepping. The contribution is both the reusable experimental capability and these specific demonstrations of what it can distinguish.

The coupled-model results must be interpreted under their stated support conditions. Supported stepping and forward progression establish functioning neural--mechanical loops, while removal of tonic drive tests a different dependence from removal of mechanical support. The balance-platform studies provide a separate hardware demonstration of a BPA-actuated limb under pseudo-vestibular inertial feedback \citep{bolen_balance_2024, bolen_selfbalancing_2024, bolen_selfbalancing_srs_2024}. Together, the simulation tools and hardware precedents support further study of balance and weight bearing without treating those capabilities as already demonstrated by the stepping tests.''','Make the whole-work discussion reflect the completed SNS contributions and distinct evidence.')
change(f,para(f,"The tools also compose across programs"),r'''The existing integrations provide a starting point for extending the model set. A full-body OpenSim model with arms and trunk would require its own geometry and actuator checks after conversion. Its controller could then be assembled or inspected with the neural block tools and evaluated with the relevant simulation interface. The matched-circuit tests and pathway-intervention procedures provide a reusable verification method, but each new model and controller combination must establish its own behavior.''','Remove unsupported claim that arbitrary full-body model transfer is already cheap, automatic, and verified.')

f='80-conclusion.tex'
change(f,para(f,'The sensory-afferent resource'),r'''The Sensory Afferent Database in Chapter~\ref{ch:database} organizes physiological evidence by properties and experimental context, making it easier to select and assess candidate feedback pathways. Chapter~\ref{ch:sns} addresses the complementary problem of testing those pathways in coupled neural and mechanical models. I developed the Li--Deng walker implementations, Gait2392-based neuromechanical models, SNS-Simscape neural blocks and actuator integrations, and staged tuning and experiment procedures. Selected demonstrations establish implementation agreement for a contact-driven rhythm core, corrective feedback in a loaded-arm model, and feedback-dependent stepping in a fixed supported W2L configuration. These results show that the tools can distinguish circuit behavior, sensory contribution, and mechanical response under specified conditions.''','State the completed tools and what their demonstrations establish.')
change(f,para(f,'The next steps are'),r'''The demonstrated contributions support controlled neuromechanical experiments with explicit neural pathways and actuator descriptions. Extending these results to unsupported whole-body behavior and hardware requires additional balance, dynamic-actuation, and transfer studies (Chapter~\ref{ch:overall}). The present findings concern the measured actuator and joint behavior and the specified simulation tests.''','Keep the scope without presenting the dissertation as unfinished integration.')
change(f,para(f,'The dissertation provides'),r'''The dissertation provides experimentally grounded actuator and joint models, a curated afferent resource, and functioning neuromechanical modeling and evaluation tools. Together, they make actuator selection, torque prediction, sensory-pathway selection, and controlled simulation tests more explicit and reproducible. These are usable contributions to biomimetic robot design and to studying how neural control interacts with mechanical structure.''','Conclude with delivered scientific and methodological contributions.')

